{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant navigating complex political landscapes and constant threats from Muslim forces. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aiming to secure and expand his county.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Melisende Countess of Edessa. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were challenging facing internal rebellions and external pressures from the Zengid dynasty led by Nur ad-Din. He demonstrated shrewd political acumen stabilizing the county and consolidating his power. He faced constant threats from Saladin's growing power in Egypt and Syria. Raymond skillfully balanced alliances with other Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem attempting to create a united front against their common enemies.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Zengids and later against Saladin. He participated in the Third Crusade though he did not directly engage in major battles. He provided logistical support and troops to the Crusader effort. His county served as a vital base of operations for Crusader forces in the Levant. He also focused on strengthening the defenses of Tripoli building fortifications to protect the city from attack. He understood the importance of maintaining a strong military to deter aggression and safeguard his territory.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his diplomatic efforts. He fostered alliances with various rulers and powers in the region. He maintained relations with the Byzantine Empire and sought support from European powers. He skillfully played rivalries between Muslim factions to his advantage. He understood that maintaining a network of alliances was crucial for survival in the volatile political environment of the Levant. His diplomatic prowess helped him to navigate treacherous situations and secure his county's interests. He often used marriage alliances to strengthen his position.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199. His death marked the end of a significant era in the history of the County of Tripoli. He was remembered as a capable and resilient ruler who successfully defended his county against numerous threats. His reign laid the groundwork for the county's continued existence for several more decades. He left behind a strong and prosperous county that continued to play a vital role in the Crusader states. His legacy is one of political skill military leadership and diplomatic acumen. He is considered one of the most important rulers of the County of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20194241404533386}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.09247366338968277}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00022420754248742014}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.1815061700181104e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.106198947643861e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.7431014789035544e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0029337245505303144}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.029779575765132904}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.0002848696312867105}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.4662635294371285e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -4.160317621426657e-05}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.000